  "theory": {
    "intro": "Producing one side of a triangle beyond a vertex creates an exterior angle. That exterior angle equals the sum of the two interior angles at the other two vertices, so a missing angle can be found in one step.",
    "facts": [
      "An **exterior angle** is formed by producing (extending) one side of a triangle beyond a vertex.",
      "Exterior angle theorem: $x+y=z$, where $z$ is the exterior angle and $x$ and $y$ are the two interior opposite angles.",
      "The two interior opposite angles are the ones at the other two vertices — **not** the interior angle next to the exterior angle.",
      "**Proof.** The angle sum of a triangle gives the interior angle beside $z$ as $180^{\\circ}-x-y$. Angles on a straight line then give $z=180^{\\circ}-(180^{\\circ}-x-y)=x+y$.",
      "An exterior angle is always larger than either of its two interior opposite angles."
    ],
    "steps": [
      "Write an equation using the exterior angle theorem.",
      "Simplify the equation.",
      "Solve for the unknown."
    ]
  },
